From: Yannick Chabanois (Ycarus) <contact@openmptcprouter.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Add outbound DSCP (IP_TOS / IPV6_TCLASS) socket option

shadowsocks-rust already lets outbound sockets be tagged for policy
routing via SO_MARK (`outbound_fwmark` / `--outbound-fwmark`), but has
no equivalent for DSCP marking.

Add a `dscp` field to `ConnectOpts` (mirroring `fwmark`) and set it via
`setsockopt(IP_TOS)` / `setsockopt(IPV6_TCLASS)` when the outbound TCP
or UDP socket is created, on Linux/Android. Plumb it through the same
paths as `outbound_fwmark`:

 - JSON config: top-level `outbound_dscp` for sslocal/ssserver/
   ssmanager, and per-server `outbound_dscp` in the `servers` array
   (both `SSConfig`/`SSServerExtConfig` and their `Config`/
   `ServerInstanceConfig` runtime counterparts).
 - CLI: `--outbound-dscp <0-255>` for sslocal, ssserver and ssmanager.

This lets an outbound shadowsocks connection be tagged with a DSCP
codepoint at connect time, e.g. so that a downstream MPTCP subflow
scheduler (such as mptcp_bpf_dscp) can pin the connection to a
particular WAN based on that DSCP value.

Setting the DSCP is best effort: a setsockopt failure is logged (the
first one as a warning, later ones at debug level) and never fails the
connect or the UDP bind, since an unmarked connection is better than
no connection. On an MPTCP socket to an IPv6 server, Linux (at least
up to 6.18) returns EOPNOTSUPP for IPV6_TCLASS but accepts IP_TOS and
stores it on the MPTCP socket, where MPTCP schedulers such as
mptcp_bpf_dscp read it; IP_TOS is set instead in that case. It does
not change the traffic class of the IPv6 packets on the wire.

Verified with `cargo check --bin sslocal --bin ssserver --bin
ssmanager --features local-redir` (clean build, no warnings).

Signed-off-by: Yannick Chabanois (Ycarus) <contact@openmptcprouter.com>
